From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 00:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] esp32: let Ctrl-C over WebREPL break a loop that never waits.

Over WebREPL, `while True: pass` cannot be interrupted, and a second
WebREPL client cannot even connect until the loop ends. Over serial the
same Ctrl-C works.

Serial Ctrl-C arrives by interrupt: uart.c and usb_serial_jtag.c call
mp_sched_keyboard_interrupt() from their ISRs, and the VM raises it at
its next branch. WebREPL's bytes arrive on a socket nobody reads until
socket_events_handler() (modsocket.c) runs the socket's callback --
os.dupterm_notify, which is where dupterm sees the interrupt character
(extmod/os_dupterm.c) -- and the port calls socket_events_handler() only
from MICROPY_EVENT_POLL_HOOK and mp_hal_delay_ms(), i.e. only while the
program sleeps, waits or idles. A loop that never waits never gets
there; the listen socket's accept callback is starved the same way.

This adds MICROPY_VM_HOOK_LOOP for the port. Every 1024 branches it
calls socket_events_vm_hook(), which, when any socket has a callback
registered and at most once per 20 ms, schedules a poll of those
sockets on a static scheduler node. The poll is the same select() and
callbacks socket_events_handler() already runs, minus its divisor.

Why scheduled rather than called from the hook: the hook runs inside
the VM on whichever thread holds the GIL, and the callbacks may be
Python (webrepl's accept handler is). Run from the scheduler, they get
the context every scheduled callback gets -- main thread only, scheduler
locked so the poll cannot nest in itself, pending exceptions raised
after it returns -- which is also the context mp_handle_pending() gives
them on the existing idle path. A static node never queues twice and
does not use a slot of MICROPY_SCHEDULER_DEPTH; one left pending over a
soft reset runs once and finds no sockets.

Cost: in running bytecode, one decrement and test per branch, and a
function call every 1024 branches that returns at once unless a socket
has a callback (i.e. WebREPL is running). With WebREPL up, a tick read
per 1024 branches and at most 50 zero-timeout select()s a second.

Code compiled with the native or viper emitters does not run VM hooks
and is not covered, as it is not covered on any other port.

Based-on: micropython 0fd6c573e (tag v1.29.0)
---
diff --git a/ports/esp32/modsocket.c b/ports/esp32/modsocket.c
index f5fd96422..9fc6fe2a2 100644
--- a/ports/esp32/modsocket.c
+++ b/ports/esp32/modsocket.c
@@ -96,6 +96,11 @@ void _socket_settimeout(socket_obj_t *sock, uint64_t timeout_ms);
 // This divisor is used to reduce the load on the system, so it doesn't poll sockets too often
 #define USOCKET_EVENTS_DIVISOR (8)
 
+// How often running bytecode polls the sockets: every this many branches, at
+// most once per this many milliseconds.
+#define SOCKET_EVENTS_VM_HOOK_DIVISOR (1024)
+#define SOCKET_EVENTS_VM_HOOK_MS (20)
+
 static uint8_t socket_events_divisor;
 static socket_obj_t *socket_events_head;
 
@@ -120,14 +125,10 @@ static void socket_events_remove(socket_obj_t *sock) {
 }
 
 // Polls all registered sockets for readability and calls their callback if they are readable
-void socket_events_handler(void) {
+static void socket_events_poll(void) {
     if (socket_events_head == NULL) {
         return;
     }
-    if (--socket_events_divisor) {
-        return;
-    }
-    socket_events_divisor = USOCKET_EVENTS_DIVISOR;
 
     fd_set rfds;
     FD_ZERO(&rfds);
@@ -153,6 +154,45 @@ void socket_events_handler(void) {
     }
 }
 
+// Called from MICROPY_EVENT_POLL_HOOK, i.e. whenever the VM waits or idles.
+void socket_events_handler(void) {
+    if (socket_events_head == NULL) {
+        return;
+    }
+    if (--socket_events_divisor) {
+        return;
+    }
+    socket_events_divisor = USOCKET_EVENTS_DIVISOR;
+    socket_events_poll();
+}
+
+// Code that never waits (eg `while True: pass`) never reaches the event poll
+// hook, so without this a WebREPL Ctrl-C would sit unread in its socket.
+// MICROPY_VM_HOOK_LOOP calls this every SOCKET_EVENTS_VM_HOOK_DIVISOR
+// branches; at most every SOCKET_EVENTS_VM_HOOK_MS it schedules a poll, which
+// then runs from the scheduler like any other scheduled callback: on the main
+// thread, with the scheduler locked, never nested inside the VM hook itself.
+uint16_t socket_events_vm_hook_count;
+static mp_sched_node_t socket_events_sched_node;
+static mp_uint_t socket_events_vm_hook_ms;
+
+static void socket_events_sched_callback(mp_sched_node_t *node) {
+    (void)node;
+    socket_events_poll();
+}
+
+void socket_events_vm_hook(void) {
+    socket_events_vm_hook_count = SOCKET_EVENTS_VM_HOOK_DIVISOR;
+    if (socket_events_head == NULL) {
+        return;
+    }
+    mp_uint_t now = mp_hal_ticks_ms();
+    if (now - socket_events_vm_hook_ms >= SOCKET_EVENTS_VM_HOOK_MS) {
+        socket_events_vm_hook_ms = now;
+        mp_sched_schedule_node(&socket_events_sched_node, socket_events_sched_callback);
+    }
+}
+
 #endif // MICROPY_PY_SOCKET_EVENTS
 
 static inline void check_for_exceptions(void) {
diff --git a/ports/esp32/mpconfigport.h b/ports/esp32/mpconfigport.h
index 45d789f46..191d9918c 100644
--- a/ports/esp32/mpconfigport.h
+++ b/ports/esp32/mpconfigport.h
@@ -342,6 +342,14 @@ void *esp_native_code_commit(void *, size_t, void *);
 
 #if MICROPY_PY_SOCKET_EVENTS
 #define MICROPY_PY_SOCKET_EVENTS_HANDLER extern void socket_events_handler(void); socket_events_handler();
+// Also poll the sockets from running bytecode, so a Ctrl-C over WebREPL can
+// break a loop that never waits. The per-branch cost is one decrement.
+extern uint16_t socket_events_vm_hook_count;
+void socket_events_vm_hook(void);
+#define MICROPY_VM_HOOK_LOOP \
+    if (--socket_events_vm_hook_count == 0) { \
+        socket_events_vm_hook(); \
+    }
 #else
 #define MICROPY_PY_SOCKET_EVENTS_HANDLER
 #endif
